  "Number": 11,
  "Question": "A merchant placed on display some dresses, each with a marked price. He then posted a sign “\\(\\dfrac13\\) off on these dresses.” The cost of the dresses was \\(\\dfrac34\\) of the price at which he actually sold them. Then the ratio of the cost to the marked price was:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac12\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac13\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac14\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac23\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac34\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "A discount of \\(\\frac13\\) leaves \\(1-\\frac13=\\frac23\\) of the marked price. Let \\(M>0\\) be the total marked price of the dresses. Their actual selling price was therefore \\(\\frac23M\\).\n\nThe cost was \\(\\frac34\\) of that selling price, so it was\n\\[\n\\frac34\\left(\\frac23M\\right)=\\frac12M.\n\\]\nDividing by the positive marked price \\(M\\), the ratio of cost to marked price is \\(\\frac12\\), choice A.",
